_rule", "label": "Classical terminology misapplied to English (the preposition-placement rule)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Latin preposition (prae+positio) literally means 'placed before'; Lowth (1762) only recommended fronting as more graceful register, not a correctness rule - the dogmatic version crowded out the scholarly nuance by the 19th century."}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_prescriptivism_and_social_class", "label": "Prescriptivism as a status marker amid 18th/19th-c. economic change", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_prescriptive_logic_arguments_tested", "label": "Prescriptive 'logic' arguments against usage, tested against real evidence", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_verb_system_combinatorial_freedom", "label": "Verb system reaches full combinatorial freedom (perfect+passive+progressive)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_progressive_perfect_semantics_current_relevance", "label": "Semantics of the progressive and the perfect ('current relevance')", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Following W.F. Twaddell: the perfect signals current relevance to the speech moment, not 'completed action' or 'nearer past' - both common alternative glosses are shown wrong by counter-examples."}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_r_lessness_sound_change", "label": "R-lessness and its ripple effects on vowel quality", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_w_rounding_fricative_lengthening_bath_trap", "label": "Rounding after /w/ and lengthening before voiceless fricatives (BATH-TRAP split)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_plain_style_scientific_writing", "label": "Triumph of scientific writing and 'the plain style'", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_general_vocabulary_expansion_mechanisms", "label": "General vocabulary expansion: borrowing plus every native word-formation mechanism", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_073_english_language_historical_intro_pt07_public_school_english_and_rp_rise", "label": "Public School English and the rise of Re